The line `visits[...]++` consists of one read and one write. When 100 goroutines do this at the same time, many may modify the map’s internal structure (buckets, resize process) simultaneously. Go’s runtime has a simple check: the map holds a “writing” flag. If a goroutine sees this flag is already set when it starts writing, the runtime halts the program immediately.

There’s a similar variant when one goroutine reads while another writes:

    `fatal error: concurrent map read and map write`

### 3.2. Why you can’t recover from it

Many people try to wrap the code with `recover`:

    `go func() {
    	defer func() {
    		if r := recover(); r != nil {
    			fmt.Println("recovered:", r)
    		}
    	}()
    	visits["user-1"]++
    }()`

This doesn’t work. `concurrent map writes` is not a `panic`. It’s a `fatal error` that the runtime throws, which terminates the entire process. `recover` can’t catch it. This choice is deliberate: if a map is corrupted, continuing to run is more dangerous than stopping.

> Note: The runtime’s detection is only “best-effort”. The program not crashing doesn’t mean your code is safe. You might just not have hit the exact moment when two goroutines collide, and your data could already be wrong silently.

### 3.3. Early detection with the race detector

A more reliable tool is the race detector, enabled with the `-race` flag:

    `go run -race main.go`

    `==================
    WARNING: DATA RACE
    Read at 0x00c000... by goroutine 8:
      runtime.mapaccess1_faststr()
      main.main.func1()
          /tmp/visits/main.go:17 +0x...

    Previous write at 0x00c000... by goroutine 7:
      runtime.mapassign_faststr()
      main.main.func1()
          /tmp/visits/main.go:17 +0x...
    ==================`

The race detector pinpoints the exact read and write lines that conflict. You should enable `-race` when running tests in CI. Programs run with `-race` are slower and use more memory, so you typically don’t use this flag for production builds.

